Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The [earlier reader review](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/pull/133#pullrequestreview-5365276527) is addressed by checking all executing mutex-dependent cores before close changes state, preserving native protection when teardown is refused, and draining operations outside the Shared lock needed by callbacks. A closing-core fence prevents fresh opens from using a draining core. Forced owner-exit, pin, unleased-snapshot and fresh-open interleavings include plain/encrypted native exclusion, external writer progress and cold indexed/sentinel checks. Facade disposal from an owned ordinary-reader callback now refuses immediately; independent leased readers and non-owning facades retain their valid behavior. The guide also identifies the last ordinary reader's `Dispose()` as an idle pin-close error path. Actual before/fixed proofs retain the original failures, intermediate race and corrected plain/encrypted harness conditions.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The [ordinary-reader follow-up](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/pull/133#pullrequestreview-5366098967) adds two corrections. Last-reader cleanup still requests native retirement but avoids joining a core that is draining the current callback, including owner-exit optional checkpointing; committed WAL remains authoritative and the concurrent disposer still receives actual close errors. Leased-reader self-disposal now refuses before mutating the cursor or disposal latch, allowing a valid retry after callback unwind. Tests retain readers strongly, prove admission release and indexed cold recovery, and cover both snapshot construction paths in plain/encrypted databases. No native lifetime is shortened and no scheduler is added.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The separate follow-up pin-loop fixture now observes startup before pin establishment, native admission, and all twenty separately committed records within its unchanged ten-second post-go budget. A live-owner check prevents owner death from mimicking yielding; held-pin controls require no committed progress. Typed retention manifests track all child PIDs and refuse copies until host and children exit, keeping files on their original temporary volume. Native crash capture also retains all 24 deliberately failed fixture sets and 20 recovery markers in controls. The original ARM failure occurred in the initial raw-file snapshot after awaited child death, before recovery reopen; its lock owner cannot be reconstructed from the missing fixture. A later green run cannot explain either original failure.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The [Shared callback review](https://github.com/JKamsker/LiteDB/pull/133#pullrequestreview-5367523337) is addressed with a synchronous, non-enlisting dependency chain. Ordinary calls retain the identity of executing handles solely to refuse waits on their native ownership; the chain restores before thread handoff and does not persist between operations. Same-file pragma getters, unrelated writes and callback nested begins fail promptly, including through another facade. Caught refusals preserve Active handles and earlier writes. Coordinated ordinary reads still return committed independent data; nested other-database work, exceptions, idle handoff and cancellation have explicit controls. The guide now tells callers to defer Shared callback work that needs the enclosing handle's native writer ownership.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	Public compatibility: typed/BSON collections, Include/vector queries, indexes and `$cols`/`$indexes` metadata are bound; read-only handles cannot write. SQL, FileStorage and maintenance are not handle APIs; drop/rename and external/system query I/O reject. Unsupported custom/coordinated/decorated providers and Shared private storage/caller streams reject handles; Direct caller streams remain supported. Existing interface implementers gain no required members. Legacy signatures remain with CS0618 warnings and a documented warnings-as-errors migration. Known committed outcomes survive cleanup errors; disposed facade access throws `ObjectDisposedException`. Parameterless Shared begin remains unbounded; opt-in timeout/cancellation bounds begin admission separately from collection `TIMEOUT`.
