Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	**The original child proof retained its Shared mode-admission lease and could retain mapped coordination participation. It also missed changed password/collation settings. The current integration fixes those differences; the current measurements above include those cleanup costs.**
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	The Shared proofs release native writer ownership between transactions. The child-wrapper cache is per originating session, and **the storage core still closes/reopens** so external writes are observed. Plain/encrypted interprocess tests verify writer progress, reuse, refreshed records and cold reopen. Retaining a coherent storage core across external writes remains a separate protocol change. Independent traces recorded 6,216 versus 9 thread-creation events for the head versus each reuse variant over about six seconds; these are event counts, not live threads or throughput. Most measured gain came from keeping the child wrapper beyond thread reuse alone.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	Inspection also corrected the nested-guard hypothesis: Dispatch's `Session.Enter()` is an EngineContext thread-static scope, not another SessionLifetime monitor. The tested scope variant removes the redundant ordinary adapter only for internal TransactionEngine calls, preserving callback isolation and one-use dispatch authorization. Broad allocation pooling was not needed for the observed Shared allocation reduction.
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	
Safety section	Check the description against the implicated contracts	**Durable-write baseline against dev:** one caller measured Direct 186.0→187.6 writes/s (+0.8% paired) and Shared 117.4→118.3 (+1.0%). Sixteen callers measured Direct −2.8% and Shared −1.1% paired. This small-insert workload does not show the large regression seen in reads. It differs from the older write table's updates to existing rows; compare within each matrix.
